{
  "dom_id": "step-q316777",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $u$ onto $v$ is the zero vector, find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since $u$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $v$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nu\u22a5v\\,\u27f9\\,u\\cdot v=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot k+2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =4k-2-15 \\\\  & =4k-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 4k-17 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =17 \\\\ k & =\\frac{17}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
